WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.896 --> 00:00:18.145
"Grandpa?" called, knocking on the study door.

2
00:00:18.165 --> 00:00:19.926
"It's dinnertime!"

3
00:00:20.506 --> 00:00:24.548
She opened the door and found Grandpa bent over his desk, as usual.

4
00:00:25.169 --> 00:00:28.030
He was an archaeologist and worked very hard.

5
00:00:28.371 --> 00:00:32.445
When he wasn't out traveling and researching, he was home in his study.

6
00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:34.666
"What are you doing, Grandpa?"

7
00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:39.349
"I’m tracing an ancient map of Mesopotamia," he said.

8
00:00:39.369 --> 00:00:47.394
"Meso-what?" Laurie peeked over his shoulder and saw a piece of tracing paper covered in wavy lines.

9
00:00:48.094 --> 00:00:51.336
Two lines were drawn in blue and made a sort of V.

10
00:00:51.356 --> 00:00:53.897
"What are those two squiggly lines?"

11
00:00:53.917 --> 00:01:02.006
"Mesopotamia was the name of a region in the Middle East, where human civilization arose thousands of years

12
00:01:02.086 --> 00:01:06.348
ago. These two lines are the Tigris and Euphrates rivers.

13
00:01:07.369 --> 00:01:14.272
Thanks to them, humans in that area had water for farming, fish to eat, and mud and reeds

14
00:01:14.332 --> 00:01:15.413
to build houses.

15
00:01:16.013 --> 00:01:18.054
You may have heard of the Sumerians.

16
00:01:18.695 --> 00:01:24.838
They were the most powerful people there—in what became known as the cradle of civilization.

17
00:01:25.458 --> 00:01:32.187
The Sumerians may have lived in mud houses, but they were very sophisticated and we owe a lot

18
00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:33.268
to their inventions.

19
00:01:33.808 --> 00:01:37.311
For example, they developed the first writing system!

20
00:01:38.492 --> 00:01:40.173
They began writing with pictures.

21
00:01:40.814 --> 00:01:44.517
So to indicate a star, they would draw a picture of a star.

22
00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:50.982
And water would be written by drawing wavy lines, like the waves in the Tigris and Euphrates.

23
00:01:51.743 --> 00:01:57.748
Later they developed those pictures into symbols that represented ideas or objects.

24
00:01:58.432 --> 00:02:03.575
Eventually they created signs to represent the sounds of their language.

25
00:02:04.856 --> 00:02:11.079
Their writing looks choppy because it was pressed into clay tablets using thin tools made from reeds.

26
00:02:11.759 --> 00:02:14.321
After that they baked the tablets in the sun.

27
00:02:14.881 --> 00:02:22.065
In addition to being masters of language, the Sumerians were clever mathematicians who tried to use numbers to

28
00:02:22.285 --> 00:02:23.946
organize the world around them.

29
00:02:24.987 --> 00:02:27.448
They were especially keen on dividing time.

30
00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:37.027
Our calendar—with a 30-day month, 7-day week, 24-hour day, 60-minute hour, and 60-second minuteㅡis based on their math!

31
00:02:37.628 --> 00:02:41.891
Can you believe we owe so much to people who lived over five thousand years ago?"

32
00:02:41.911 --> 00:02:47.496
"No, but I can believe Mom will be mad at us if we don't go downstairs and eat

33
00:02:47.536 --> 00:02:49.197
dinner," Laurie said.

34
00:02:49.217 --> 00:02:58.253
"Your mother is like the great King Hammurabi of ancient Babylonia, which was an area of Mesopotamia," Grandpa

35
00:02:58.313 --> 00:03:01.395
said. "She lays down the laws around here!"

36
00:03:01.415 --> 00:03:03.697
"I've heard of him!

37
00:03:04.278 --> 00:03:09.062
Wasn't he the scary king who asked for 'an eye for an eye, and a tooth for a

38
00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:11.124
tooth'?" Laurie shuddered.

39
00:03:11.144 --> 00:03:15.547
"Yes, I guess we might say he sounds scary now.

40
00:03:16.048 --> 00:03:22.734
But Hammurabi's laws kept society in order at a time when there was lots of fighting between tribes.

41
00:03:23.434 --> 00:03:30.478
I think his laws are interesting because they teach us about what was considered important in ancient Mesopotamia.

42
00:03:31.539 --> 00:03:37.941
For example, we know that people were expected to take responsibility for their work and actions.

43
00:03:38.501 --> 00:03:43.724
If a house collapsed and killed the people inside, the builder was put to death.

44
00:03:44.524 --> 00:03:49.206
But unfortunately, punishment wasn't always as clear-cut as it sounds.

45
00:03:49.686 --> 00:03:53.948
You were often able to escape a harsh sentence by paying a large fine.

46
00:03:54.524 --> 00:03:57.345
In ancient Babylonia it helped to be wealthy.

47
00:03:58.406 --> 00:04:00.987
Would you like to see pictures of Babylonia's capital?

48
00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:06.329
It was called Babylon and was one of the most impressive cities in Mesopotamia.

49
00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:09.431
Those huge structures are called ziggurats.

50
00:04:10.031 --> 00:04:12.532
They were built entirely out of mud bricks.

51
00:04:13.112 --> 00:04:15.353
They were probably very difficult to build.

52
00:04:15.854 --> 00:04:21.016
People took great care in building them, however, because they were made for worshipping gods.

53
00:04:22.196 --> 00:04:26.138
The steep sides were stepped and covered with beautiful gardens.

54
00:04:26.659 --> 00:04:28.360
At the top was a shrine.

55
00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:34.083
The most famous ziggurat is the temple to the god Marduk in the city of Ur.

56
00:04:34.603 --> 00:04:37.525
You recall the Tower of Babel from the Bible, don't you?

57
00:04:38.045 --> 00:04:43.569
In that story people tried to build a tower that would reach the heavens, but they failed in

58
00:04:43.609 --> 00:04:44.009
the end.

59
00:04:44.589 --> 00:04:49.032
Some believe the ziggurat in Ur is the basis of the biblical legend.

60
00:04:49.824 --> 00:04:55.686
The city of Ur, which is in present-day Iraq, is thought to connect to other stories from the

61
00:04:55.746 --> 00:04:56.526
Bible as well.

62
00:04:57.206 --> 00:05:04.108
It was the home of Abraham, and when archaeologists first discovered the ruins of Ur in the 1800s,

63
00:05:04.728 --> 00:05:10.430
they found evidence of a great flood that had likely wiped out most of the city.

64
00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:15.651
They believe this may have been the basis of the great flood story in the Bible as well.

65
00:05:16.331 --> 00:05:19.132
You know, the one that led to Noah building his ark.

66
00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:20.861
Wait a minute.

67
00:05:21.301 --> 00:05:24.243
I have something to show you from the excavation at Ur.

68
00:05:24.924 --> 00:05:31.788
A British archaeologist uncovered some of the grandest treasures of the ancient world when he found the temples

69
00:05:32.108 --> 00:05:35.391
and cemetery there in the 1920s and ’30s.

70
00:05:36.111 --> 00:05:41.035
The tomb of Lady Puabi, for example, was full of beautiful things.

71
00:05:41.695 --> 00:05:48.079
This royal woman was buried with all her riches, and was covered from head to foot in jewels

72
00:05:48.159 --> 00:05:48.740
and gold.

73
00:05:49.812 --> 00:05:54.574
Lady Puabi's real headdress is in a museum, but I have a replica.

74
00:05:55.094 --> 00:05:59.816
A replica is something that's made to look exactly like the real thing.

75
00:06:00.296 --> 00:06:02.617
Try this on for size, Lady Laurie."

76
00:06:03.218 --> 00:06:11.761
Grandpa leaned over and placed a delicate headdress decorated with dangling golden leaves, rings, and flowers on Laurie's

77
00:06:11.801 --> 00:06:18.124
head. "Laurie, didn't I send you to call Grandpa for dinner ten minutes ago?"

78
00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:20.002
Laurie's mom shouted.

79
00:06:20.022 --> 00:06:22.647
"What are you two doing up there?

80
00:06:23.007 --> 00:06:24.430
Dinner is getting cold!"

81
00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:27.114
"That's Lady Laurie to you, Mom!"

82
00:06:27.535 --> 00:06:28.216
Laurie said.

83
00:06:28.737 --> 00:06:32.603
She turned on her heel and strode out the door, her headdress jingling.
